    <item>
      <title>Go Mechanics: Data-Oriented Design and Semantics</title>
      <link>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-data-oriented-design/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 01 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-data-oriented-design/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In Go, understanding data-oriented design and choosing between value and pointer semantics is fundamental to writing maintainable, performant software. Here&amp;rsquo;s a comprehensive summary of Bill Kennedy&amp;rsquo;s design philosophy on data and semantics from Ardan Labs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;design-philosophies-the-foundation&#34;&gt;Design Philosophies: The Foundation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;value-vs-pointer-semantics&#34;&gt;Value vs. Pointer Semantics&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;Value semantics keep values on the stack, which reduces pressure on the Garbage Collector (GC). However, value semantics require various copies of any given value to be stored, tracked and maintained. Pointer semantics place values on the heap, which can put pressure on the GC. However, pointer semantics are efficient because only one value needs to be stored, tracked and maintained.&amp;rdquo; - Bill Kennedy&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Mechanics: Memory Profiling and Performance Optimization</title>
      <link>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-memory-profiling/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 01 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-memory-profiling/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In Go, understanding memory allocations and their performance impact is crucial for writing efficient code. Here&amp;rsquo;s a comprehensive summary of Bill Kennedy&amp;rsquo;s core principles on Memory Profiling from the Ardan Labs series.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-profiling-process&#34;&gt;The Profiling Process&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Memory profiling in Go helps identify where allocations are happening and why values escape to the heap. This is essential for performance optimization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;key-tools&#34;&gt;Key Tools&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Benchmarking&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;go test -bench&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;-benchmem&lt;/code&gt; flag&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory Profiling&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;go test -memprofile&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;pprof&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;go tool pprof -alloc_space&lt;/code&gt; for analysis&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Compiler Reporting&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;go build -gcflags &amp;quot;-m -m&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; for escape analysis&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-case-study-string-replacement-algorithm&#34;&gt;The Case Study: String Replacement Algorithm&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;the-problem&#34;&gt;The Problem&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Create a function that finds &amp;ldquo;elvis&amp;rdquo; in a byte stream and replaces it with &amp;ldquo;Elvis&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Mechanics: Understanding Slices and Internals</title>
      <link>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-slices/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 01 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://ths-dev-works.github.io/posts/go-slices/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In Go, slices are one of the most powerful and frequently used data structures. They provide a flexible and efficient way to work with sequences of data. Here&amp;rsquo;s a comprehensive summary of slice mechanics based on the official Go blog and Ardan Labs insights.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;arrays-the-foundation&#34;&gt;Arrays: The Foundation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before understanding slices, we must understand arrays. Arrays in Go are fixed-size collections with the size being part of the type.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;array-properties&#34;&gt;Array Properties&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fixed Size&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;[4]int&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;[5]int&lt;/code&gt; to be different, incompatible types&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Value Type&lt;/strong&gt;: Arrays to be values, not pointers to first element&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory Layout&lt;/strong&gt;: Elements laid out sequentially in memory&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Copy Semantics&lt;/strong&gt;: Assignment to create a full copy of the array&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;// Array declarations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kd&#34;&gt;var&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;// i == 1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;// Array literals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Penn&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Teller&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nx&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;kt&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Penn&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Teller&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;// Compiler counts elements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h2 id=&#34;slices-the-abstraction&#34;&gt;Slices: The Abstraction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slices build on arrays to provide flexibility and power. A slice is a &lt;strong&gt;descriptor&lt;/strong&gt; of an array segment.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
